Team Roles & Responsibilities (Simplified)
Overview

6 team members, each with a clear focus. No need to understand all the code — each person owns one piece.

Role 1: Project Coordinator

What they own: Project structure, team workflow, automation
What they do:

Create the main e can run the project locally without issues
Ensure code revieproject folder and organize it into sections
Set up rules for how team members share code (branching, merging)
Create automated checks that run every time someone submits work (catches mistakes early)
Write a guide: "How to set up this project on your computer"
Make sure everyonw happens before work is merged into the main project

Who they work with: Everyone (acts as coordinator)

Role 2: Backend/Server Developer

What they own: The application server that receives requests
What they do:

Build the server application that listens for requests from users
Create the main entry point where all requests come in
Set up 3 main endpoints:
"Generate a map" endpoint (receives parameters, calls algorithm people, returns map data)
"Export as image" endpoint (gets map data, passes to export person)
"Export as JSON" endpoint (gets map data, passes to export person)
Define what information each endpoint expects and what it sends back
Write tests to verify each endpoint works correctly
Handle errors gracefully (if something fails, show a helpful message)

Who they work with:

Algorithm developer (calls their functions)
Export developer (sends them data)
Frontend developer (sends them requests)
Role 3: Procedural Generation Developer

What they own: The map generation algorithms
What they do:

Write 3 different ways to generate random maps:
Perlin Noise method (smooth, natural-looking terrain)
Cellular Automata method (cave-like, organic structures)
Binary Space Partition method (structured, room-based dungeons)
Each algorithm takes in parameters (map size, randomness level, etc.)
Each algorithm outputs a 2D grid of tiles with their types
Ensure the same seed always produces the same map (reproducibility)
Test edge cases (very small maps, very large maps, extreme parameters)
Write documentation explaining how each algorithm works

Who they work with:

Backend developer (provides parameters, receives tile data)
Role 4: Export/Data Developer

What they own: Converting maps into downloadable files
What they do:

Take the generated map (2D grid of tiles) and convert it to a visual image
Create color definitions (what color is grass? water? mountain?)
Take the generated map and convert it to a JSON file (text format)
Handle different export sizes (small image, large image)
Write tests to verify images are created correctly
Write tests to verify JSON files are valid

Who they work with:

Backend developer (receives tile data)
Role 5: Frontend/UI Developer

What they own: The website interface users interact with
What they do:

Design and build the webpage users see
Create a dropdown menu to pick which algorithm to use
Create sliders for parameters (map size, scale, complexity, etc.)
Create a "Generate" button
Show a preview of the generated map
Create "Download as Image" button
Create "Download as JSON" button
Connect buttons to the server (send requests, receive maps)
Render the map on screen from server data
Handle loading states (show "generating..." while map is being created)

Who they work with:

Backend developer (sends requests, receives data)
Role 6: Quality & Documentation Lead

What they do:

Test the entire system end-to-end (generate → export → download, all working together)
Verify that each team member's work doesn't break others' work
Write a setup guide for new developers
Write user guide (how to use the application)
Write API documentation (what each endpoint does, what data it needs)
Write system architecture overview (big picture of how everything fits together)
Write guide explaining each algorithm in plain language
Measure how much of the code is tested
Create a quality checklist before any release

Who they work with: Everyone (coordinates with all roles)

Simple Workflow
Coordinator sets up the project folder and rules
Algorithm Dev writes the 3 map generation methods
Backend Dev creates the server that calls algorithms
Export Dev adds image/JSON conversion
Frontend Dev builds the webpage
QA Lead tests everything and writes documentation
Communication
Each person works on their piece independently
Share interfaces clearly (Backend tells Export: "I'll send you tiles like this")
Code review before merging (at least one other person checks your work)
Weekly sync: 10 min check-in on blockers
Success Criteria

✅ Each role has clear, isolated responsibilities
✅ Beginner can understand what they're building without knowing all details
✅ Easy to parallelize work (people don't step on each other)
✅ Easy to replace tech stack later (logic stays the same)